from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer
from sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB
messages = [
  "Win a free prize now",
  "Claim your free reward",
  "Congratulations you won cash",
  "Get a special offer today",
  "Please attend the meeting tomorrow",
  "Can we meet after class",
  "Your assignment is due Friday",
  "Please send me the notes"
]
labels = [
  "Spam",
  "Spam",
  "Spam",
  "Spam",
  "Not Spam",
  "Not Spam",
  "Not Spam",
  "Not Spam"
]
vectorizer = CountVectorizer()
X = vectorizer.fit_transform(messages)
model = MultinomialNB()
model.fit(X, labels)
new_message = ["Win cash today"]
new_X = vectorizer.transform(new_message)
prediction = model.predict(new_X)
print("Message:", new_message[0])
print("Prediction:", prediction[0])
user_message = input("Enter a message: ")
user_X = vectorizer.transform([user_message])
prediction = model.predict(user_X)
print("Prediction:", prediction[0])

OUTPUT:
Message:   Win cash today
Prediction: Spam
Enter a message: Please send me notes
Prediction: Not spam
CONCLUSION:
Thus, the spam message detection system was successfully implemented in Python using the
Multinomial Naive Bayes algorithm. The program converts text messages into numerical features
using CountVectorizer and classifies them as spam or not spam. This practical demonstrates the
application of Natural Language Processing (NLP), text classification, and supervised machine
learning in Artificial Intelligence.
